= WiiLand Plan =

Status: active migration plan for distributions where Wayland is the primary
session and Xorg input drivers are legacy compatibility only.

== Decision ==

The default Wayland path is a Linux uinput bridge, shipped as wiilandd and
exposed explicitly as backend=uinput/--backend uinput. It consumes
hid-wiimote/libxwiimote compatibility-library events and creates virtual evdev
devices. Wayland compositors already ingest those devices through libinput and
native game stacks already ingest them through evdev, SDL, Wine, and Proton.

This is intentionally not an XWayland or Xorg input-driver port. Wayland clients
cannot generally inject input into other clients. Compositor-specific virtual
input protocols are not portable enough for the default backend. libei is the
preferred future compositor-mediated backend once it is broadly available; it
will be an optional backend, not a replacement for the uinput baseline.

== Current shipped behavior ==

wiilandd:
  - monitors hid-wiimote devices through libxwiimote compatibility library;
  - creates one virtual uinput gamepad per Wii Remote by default;
  - optionally creates a virtual desktop mouse/keyboard with --profile desktop
    or --profile both;
  - forwards core, Nunchuk, Classic Controller, Pro Controller, Guitar, and
    Drums button events to the gamepad profile;
  - forwards accelerometer, MotionPlus, Nunchuk, Classic Controller,
    Pro Controller, Guitar, Drums, and Balance Board axes to the gamepad
    profile;
  - maps Wii Remote core input in the desktop profile: D-pad repeat and valid
    IR events move the pointer, A/B click, +/- send Enter/Escape, Home sends
    the compositor overview key, and 1/2 send Page Down/Page Up;
  - supports key=value configuration for default profile, syspath and devtype
    profile overrides, desktop button actions, D-pad pointer speed, IR pointer
    gain, IR jitter deadzone, and IR smoothing
    through /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf, ~/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf, or
    --config; res/wiilandd.conf is the distributable sample;
  - provides --self-test for deterministic profile, button-map, config, and
    pointer-motion validation without hardware;
  - ships a CI smoke harness that compiles wiilandd against committed
    libxwiimote stubs and runs --self-test, --check-config, and --dump-config;
  - provides --dry-run --trace-events for hardware validation reports without
    creating virtual input devices;
  - handles hotplug and extension changes without Xorg configuration.

Required runtime permissions:
  - normal input read access for hid-wiimote evdev nodes;
  - write access to /dev/uinput, normally via a distribution udev rule or a
    service running with the input/uinput capability policy. The sample service
    validates configuration with --check-config before starting.

== Roadmap ==

1. Desktop profile
   Shipped as --profile desktop and --profile both with D-pad motion, IR
   pointer motion, configurable desktop button actions, and IR pointer gain.
   Next refinement is optional smoothing/deadzones for IR pointer motion.

2. Per-device policy
   Shipped as device.<syspath-substring>.profile and
   device-type.<devtype-substring>.profile rules for choosing gamepad, desktop,
   or both per controller path/type. Bluetooth-address matching remains a future
   refinement if hardware reports show a stable identifier surface worth
   standardizing.

3. Optional libei backend
   Add --backend=libei when libei and compositor support are common enough.
   libei gives user-mediated emulated input through a compositor EIS endpoint;
   uinput remains the fallback because it works across compositors and native
   game stacks today.

4. Distribution integration
   Automake installs the uinput udev rule, systemd user service, and sample
   config from res/. Keep the legacy Xorg blacklist file for legacy Xorg
   sessions only.

5. Test hardware matrix
   Completion requires direct reports for:
   - original Wii Remote: core buttons, accelerometer, IR desktop pointer;
   - Wii Remote Plus: core buttons, accelerometer, MotionPlus axes;
   - Nunchuk: stick, C/Z buttons, accelerometer passthrough;
   - Classic Controller: sticks, triggers, face buttons;
   - Wii U Pro Controller: sticks, triggers, face buttons;
   - Guitar: fret buttons, strum bar, whammy, stick;
   - Drums: pad hits, cymbal/tom pressure axes, bass and hi-hat pedals;
   - Balance Board: all four weight sensors.

   Each report must record kernel version, libxwiimote version, wiilandd
   --version output, git commit, compositor, distribution, Bluetooth adapter,
   and whether SDL, Wine/Proton, and native Wayland desktop profile behavior
   matched expectations.

   Recommended capture sequence:
     tools/wiilandd-hardware-report.sh
     tools/wiilandd-hardware-report.sh N

   The first command records finite host, config, version, and device-list
   diagnostics. The second command captures monotonic-timestamped
   --dry-run --trace-events --verbose output for one device; use the number from
   --list or a sysfs path.

   After dry-run traces show expected events, repeat without --dry-run and
   validate the virtual gamepad in an SDL input tester, one Wine/Proton game,
   and the desktop pointer/buttons under a native Wayland compositor. Do not
   mark the Wayland migration complete until this matrix has real hardware
   evidence.
